1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,452
Hi, this is Brightloop, a subscription management system built for the Odoo and FLUX Lucknow hackathon.

2
00:00:06,452 --> 00:00:16,913
It runs a subscription business end to end: products and plans, quotation to subscription, automatic recurring invoicing, discounts, payments, and a finance dashboard.

3
00:00:16,913 --> 00:00:23,552
It's a custom Node and PostgreSQL backend with a React front end, no static data, no backend-as-a-service.

4
00:00:23,552 --> 00:00:27,624
Everything you'll see is read from or written to a real database.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,934
Subscriptions look simple, and aren't.

6
00:00:31,934 --> 00:00:37,647
You have to bill every customer, every period, never twice, never skipped.

7
00:00:37,647 --> 00:00:43,438
You have to apply the right discount and the right tax at the right moment.

8
00:00:43,438 --> 00:00:52,471
Every subscription has to move through a strict lifecycle, and your finance numbers have to stay honest in real time.

9
00:00:52,471 --> 00:01:06,368
Brightloop solves all of that on a real relational database, with three things at its core: idempotent recurring billing, automatic discounts and tax, and proper role-based access.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,754
Architecturally it's a clean three-tier app.

11
00:01:10,754 --> 00:01:14,823
The React single-page app calls the API with a JWT in the header.

12
00:01:14,823 --> 00:01:27,717
The Express API runs every request through the same pipeline: authenticate, check the role, validate the body with Zod, then a thin route hands off to a transactional service, which calls pure domain rules.

13
00:01:27,717 --> 00:01:32,787
Those domain rules and the invoice maths are unit-tested with no database at all.

14
00:01:32,787 --> 00:01:41,237
And PostgreSQL does the heavy lifting: parameterised SQL everywhere, row locks where it matters, and SQL views that feed the dashboard.

15
00:01:41,237 --> 00:01:43,928
Business logic never leaks into the routes.

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,839
Let's log in as admin and set up the catalogue.

17
00:01:48,839 --> 00:01:50,531
Products can carry variants.

18
00:01:50,531 --> 00:01:59,049
Here a Brand equals Odoo variant adds five hundred and sixty rupees to the price, so the final price becomes two thousand five hundred sixty.

19
00:01:59,049 --> 00:02:07,870
Plans set the billing cadence: daily, weekly, monthly, yearly, along with per-plan flags like auto-close and pause that decide what's legal later.

20
00:02:07,870 --> 00:02:11,857
And I've armed a discount: ten percent off for five or more seats.

21
00:02:11,857 --> 00:02:13,367
I don't apply it by hand.

22
00:02:13,367 --> 00:02:19,288
It attaches itself to the next invoice automatically, and stops the moment it expires or runs out.

23
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,167
Now a subscription.

24
00:02:29,167 --> 00:02:37,890
I create it from a quotation template, so the plan and the line items come in automatically with a live price preview, then I pick a customer.

25
00:02:37,890 --> 00:02:41,514
Watch the status move: Draft, Quotation, Confirmed, Active.

26
00:02:41,514 --> 00:02:46,183
The important part is that the buttons only ever offer the legal next state.

27
00:02:46,183 --> 00:02:49,377
This is a real state machine enforced on the server.

28
00:02:49,377 --> 00:02:52,817
If you try an illegal transition, the API returns a 409.

29
00:02:52,817 --> 00:02:57,424
The UI isn't just hiding buttons; the database-backed rules won't allow it.

30
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,809
This is the signature moment. I hit Run Billing.

31
00:03:10,809 --> 00:03:20,466
The system finds every active subscription that's due and creates one invoice each, four hundred twenty-nine invoices, with the discount and the tax already applied.

32
00:03:20,466 --> 00:03:22,983
Now watch what happens when I run it again.

33
00:03:22,983 --> 00:03:24,270
Zero invoices created.

34
00:03:24,270 --> 00:03:26,670
Four hundred twenty-nine periods skipped.

35
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:28,425
Nothing is billed twice, ever.

36
00:03:28,425 --> 00:03:39,311
And that guarantee isn't in the app code, it's in the database: a unique constraint on subscription and period, plus insert on conflict do nothing, backed by a per-subscription row lock.

37
00:03:39,311 --> 00:03:42,998
Run it ten times, you still get exactly one invoice per period.

38
00:03:42,998 --> 00:03:45,632
Missed periods get caught up on the next run.

39
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,394
Payments close the loop.

40
00:03:55,394 --> 00:04:01,843
I open an invoice and record a part payment, fourteen hundred rupees against a balance of twenty-eight sixteen.

41
00:04:01,843 --> 00:04:04,806
The invoice stays Confirmed with a balance showing.

42
00:04:04,806 --> 00:04:08,582
Then I record the rest, and the invoice flips to Paid on its own.

43
00:04:08,582 --> 00:04:10,906
Paid is never a status you can just set.

44
00:04:10,906 --> 00:04:14,857
It's only reachable by recording real payments that cover the total.

45
00:04:14,857 --> 00:04:18,168
And if you try to overpay, the API rejects it with a 409.

46
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,959
Access control is strict, and it's three-tiered.

47
00:04:30,959 --> 00:04:33,424
Admin owns the catalogue and adds staff.

48
00:04:33,424 --> 00:04:39,404
Staff do the daily work: subscriptions, invoices, billing, payments, but can't touch admin setup.

49
00:04:39,404 --> 00:04:46,308
Customers only ever see their own subscriptions and invoices; hit a staff route as a customer and you get a 403.

50
00:04:46,308 --> 00:04:54,013
Validation is just as strict: try a weak password and you get a clear, field-level error from both the client and the server.

51
00:04:54,013 --> 00:05:05,848
And when I sign in as the customer, the admin screens simply aren't there, enforced by a requireRole check that re-runs on every single request, so deactivating a user cuts them off instantly.

52
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,396
Back as staff, the finance dashboard.

53
00:05:10,396 --> 00:05:17,261
KPI cards for active subscriptions, monthly recurring revenue, revenue collected, outstanding and overdue.

54
00:05:17,261 --> 00:05:25,357
A revenue-by-month chart, an overdue table oldest-first, and CSV export, all driven by SQL views, not computed in JavaScript.

55
00:05:25,357 --> 00:05:33,129
And this isn't a toy dataset: it's running over roughly five thousand seeded subscriptions and twelve thousand invoices.

56
00:05:33,129 --> 00:05:36,691
Every heavy list page responds in tens of milliseconds.

57
00:05:36,691 --> 00:05:39,152
The requirement was under two seconds.

58
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,499
Let me show you the running product itself.

59
00:05:54,499 --> 00:05:59,148
I sign in as admin at the live URL, and I'm straight into the finance dashboard.

60
00:05:59,148 --> 00:06:01,822
These are real numbers coming from PostgreSQL.

61
00:06:01,822 --> 00:06:06,936
Over to Subscriptions: every status chip you see comes from that enforced state machine.

62
00:06:06,936 --> 00:06:11,062
Into Invoices, and this is the one-click moment, Run Billing for today.

63
00:06:11,062 --> 00:06:18,675
Four hundred twenty-nine invoices created in one pass, each with tax and discount already applied; run it again and you'd get zero.

64
00:06:18,675 --> 00:06:20,303
Finally the customer portal.

65
00:06:20,303 --> 00:06:26,056
Signed in as a customer, they see only their own subscription and their own invoices, nothing else.

66
00:06:26,056 --> 00:06:31,984
Same codebase, deployed live on Render with a managed PostgreSQL database, behind a single public URL.

67
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,656
None of this is just syntax-checked, it's verified end to end against a live PostgreSQL.

68
00:06:53,656 --> 00:07:00,598
Forty-seven tests pass: thirty-five unit and access-control tests, twelve database-backed integration tests.

69
00:07:00,598 --> 00:07:06,512
Billing idempotency is proven: first run four hundred twenty-nine invoices, second run zero.

70
00:07:06,512 --> 00:07:07,733
Role scoping holds.

71
00:07:07,733 --> 00:07:12,361
Lists over five thousand rows come back in twenty to forty milliseconds.

72
00:07:12,361 --> 00:07:19,560
And it's deployed live on Render with its own managed PostgreSQL, behind one public URL, from the same codebase.

73
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:35,929
So that's Brightloop: a custom relational backend with real dynamic data, enforced state machines, idempotent billing, automatic discounts and tax, role-based access, and a finance dashboard, all verified end to end and running live.

74
00:07:35,929 --> 00:07:37,125
Thanks for watching.

75
00:07:37,125 --> 00:07:41,728
The live demo and admin login are on screen if you'd like to try it yourself.
